    $astroMoons_master$> The gas giants chapter ended on a hook: a planet massive enough to form beyond the snow line does not merely become a planet, it captures a disk of its own, and out of that disk it builds a system. This chapter takes that hook seriously. The regular moons of Jupiter and Saturn were not swept up at random; they condensed, in orbit, out of a flattened rotating cloud of gas and dust that was in every important respect a miniature of the disk that built the planets. That single fact turns each giant into a scale model of planet formation that we can walk up to and inspect. And Jupiter, generously, ran the cleanest experiment in the solar system on our behalf: four large moons, all at one planet, all the same age, differing mainly in how far out they formed and how hard they are squeezed today. Line them up and their densities fall smoothly outward, from a bone-dry ball of rock to a half-frozen world of ice — the same rock-then-ice arrangement the solar system itself shows, reproduced in a body 5 astronomical units wide. Composition is written by where a moon formed; activity is written by how it is being pulled now. Hold those two axes apart and the whole zoo of giant-planet satellites falls into order.

## Where almost all the moons are

Begin with a census, because it reframes the question before you ask it. Count the moons of the solar system and you find them overwhelmingly in four places. Jupiter has of order ninety-five confirmed satellites; Saturn, after the survey campaigns of the last few years, has crossed one hundred and forty. Uranus has close to thirty, Neptune around sixteen. The terrestrial worlds contribute a rounding error: Earth's one, Mars's two captured splinters, and nothing else — Mercury and Venus are bare.

Add the giants' satellites up and they account for something like **ninety-nine percent of every moon in the solar system.** The pattern is not subtle and it is not an accident. Moons in this abundance are what you get when a body is massive enough to hold onto a disk of its own during formation, and only the giant planets ever were. The inner planets formed inside the snow line from a thin scattering of rock, too small and too warm to keep a gas disk; whatever moons they might have spun off were lost, and the two moons they do keep — Luna and the Martian pair — arrived by other routes, a giant impact and a probable capture, that the terrestrial chapters treat in their own right.

So the moons are not scattered evenly. They are pooled around the four largest planets, in numbers that track planetary mass, in systems that look — once you learn to see them this way — like solar systems in miniature. That resemblance is the organising idea of everything that follows, and it is worth making precise rather than poetic.

### A disk around a planet is a solar system in miniature

When Jupiter finished its runaway accretion, it was not a finished ball. It was a hot, luminous, still-collapsing object surrounded by a **circumplanetary disk** — gas and dust in orbit around it, fed by material still spiralling in from the solar nebula, flattened by rotation and warmed from the centre by the young planet's own heat. Everything that built the planets from the Sun's disk operated again here, one tier down: condensation of solids set by local temperature, accretion of those solids into larger bodies, migration, resonance, and a central object whose luminosity draws a temperature gradient across the whole structure.

That is why a giant planet is a scale model and not merely a collection. The leading picture for the Galilean satellites, the **gas-starved disk** model of Canup and Ward, has them growing slowly in a tenuous, continuously resupplied disk — slowly enough that they did not migrate all the way into the planet and be lost, fast enough that they formed at all. The details are argued over, but the frame is secure: the regular moons are disk children. They orbit prograde (the same sense the planet spins), close to the planet's equatorial plane, on near-circular orbits — the three signatures of having condensed in place rather than been grabbed from passing traffic. The captured moons, the ones on tilted, eccentric, sometimes backward orbits far out, are a different population with a different history, and Neptune's stolen giant Triton is their most spectacular member; they get their own chapter, and this one sets them aside deliberately. Here the subject is the disk-born regulars, because they are the ones that carry the record of formation.

{{image: Galilean moons | The four Galilean satellites to scale, in order of distance from Jupiter: Io, Europa, Ganymede, Callisto. Same planet, same age, formed in the same circumplanetary disk — a controlled experiment in how a moon's composition is set by where it condensed.}}

## Jupiter's controlled experiment

Here is the gift Jupiter hands us. Four large moons, discovered by Galileo in January 1610 and named for the loves of Zeus — Io, Europa, Ganymede, Callisto — orbit the same planet, formed at the same time, out of the same disk. In an experiment you want to vary one thing and hold everything else fixed. Nature has very nearly done that. The four moons share a parent, an age, and a formation mechanism. What differs between them, principally, is **how far from Jupiter they formed** — and, as a separate axis we will come to, how hard Jupiter squeezes them today. Almost everything else is controlled.

So look at what varies, and the cleanest number to look at is bulk density. Density is the first thing you can measure about a moon that is not just its picture: get its size from how large it looks and its mass from how it tugs a passing spacecraft, and the ratio tells you what it is made of. Here are the four:

| Moon | Orbital radius (10³ km) | Radius (km) | Density (g/cm³) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Io** | 422 | 1,822 | **3.53** |
| **Europa** | 671 | 1,561 | **3.01** |
| **Ganymede** | 1,070 | 2,634 | **1.94** |
| **Callisto** | 1,883 | 2,410 | **1.83** |

Read that density column from top to bottom and it does something remarkable: it **falls, monotonically, with distance from Jupiter.** The moon closest in is denser than solid rock; the moon farthest out is barely denser than the ice on a winter pond. And it does so smoothly, the same way the solar system's own densities fall as you walk outward from the rocky inner planets to the ice-rich outer worlds. One planet, four moons, a density gradient that echoes the whole solar system in miniature. This is the spine of the chapter, and it has a one-line explanation.

### The snow line, drawn again around a single planet

Rock condenses out of a cooling gas at high temperature; water ice condenses only where it is cold enough, below about 150 kelvin. In the Sun's disk that threshold defined the snow line near 2.7 AU, and it is the reason the inner planets are dry rock and the outer solar system is loaded with ice — the framework the gas giants chapter leaned on. The proto-Jupiter drew a snow line of its very own, across its own disk, for exactly the same reason: it was hot.

A newly formed Jupiter radiated the enormous gravitational energy of its assembly. Close in, its disk was too warm for water ice to condense — solids there were anhydrous silicate rock and metal, nothing more. Farther out the disk cooled below the ice point, and water condensed in bulk, doubling or better the mass of solid material available to build a moon. So a moon that grew in the inner disk is made of rock and got little or no water; a moon that grew in the cold outer disk is made of roughly half ice by volume. Io formed inside Jupiter's snow line and is bone-dry rock. Callisto formed well outside it and is half ice. Europa and Ganymede straddle the transition. The density gradient is not a coincidence to be explained away — it is a **thermometer**, a fossil record of the temperature profile of Jupiter's disk 4.5 billion years ago, read off four moons in a row.

That is the whole argument in one breath: **composition is set by formation distance.** A young hot Jupiter arranged its moons the way a young hot Sun arranged its planets, rock inside and ice outside, and the density sequence is the arrangement made visible. Everything else in this chapter is commentary on that sentence, or on the second axis — activity — that runs across it.

### The four moons, one at a time

The density column tells the compositional story; the moons individually tell what that composition does, and how a completely separate agent, tidal heating, cuts across the pattern.

**Io**, density 3.53, is the density of the experiment's control for "pure rock" — no ice, no water, essentially a differentiated ball of silicate and iron with a partially molten interior. It should, by rights, be a cold dead rock like our own Moon, which it resembles in size and bulk. It is instead **the most volcanically active body in the solar system**, resurfacing itself so fast that it has no impact craters at all, throwing plumes hundreds of kilometres above its surface, painting itself in sulfur yellows and reds. Nothing about its *composition* explains that; a ball of rock this size formed cold and should have stayed cold. The explanation is tidal — Io is racked and kneaded by Jupiter's gravity along its slightly eccentric orbit, and the flexing dissipates staggering amounts of heat. That mechanism is the whole subject of the tidal-heating chapter and is not re-derived here; what matters for the comparative picture is the logical split it forces. Io's *makeup* is a formation fact; Io's *fury* is a present-tense tidal fact. The two have nothing to do with each other, and Io is the moon that proves you must keep them apart.

{{image: Io (moon) | Io, a ball of dry rock the size of our Moon, resurfaced continuously by the most intense volcanism in the solar system. Its composition was set by forming inside Jupiter's snow line; its violence is set entirely by tidal heating — the two axes of this chapter, on one body.}}

**Europa**, density 3.01, is rock with a veneer of water — a silicate world wrapped in an outer shell of water ice perhaps 100 kilometres thick, a few percent of the moon's mass. Under that ice, the tidal flexing keeps a **global ocean of liquid water** in contact with a rocky floor. Europa is the flagship of the ocean-worlds story, and the case for its ocean, and what a rock-floored ocean might mean for life, belong to that chapter. For the comparative set the point is narrower: Europa sits exactly where its density predicts, between the dry rock of Io and the icy worlds beyond, having formed near Jupiter's snow line where it got some water but not the full ration. Its ocean, like Io's volcanism, is a tidal gift laid on top of a compositional inheritance.

**Ganymede**, density 1.94, is the largest moon in the solar system — larger, in raw diameter, than the planet Mercury, and larger than Titan by a whisker. It is roughly half rock and half ice by mass, cleanly **differentiated** into a metallic core, a rock mantle, and thick ice layers above, with its own subsurface ocean sandwiched between ice phases. It also does something no other moon does, which earns it a section of its own below. Ganymede formed comfortably outside Jupiter's snow line, took on its full share of water, and had enough internal heat during formation to sort itself into layers.

{{image: Ganymede (moon) | Ganymede, the largest moon in the solar system and bigger across than the planet Mercury. Half rock and half ice, fully differentiated into core, mantle and ice shells, it is the only moon known to generate its own magnetic field.}}

**Callisto**, density 1.83, is the outermost and the most quietly informative of the four. Its density says half ice, like Ganymede — but its *interior* is different, and the difference is the whole point of calling this an experiment. Where Ganymede is cleanly layered, Callisto appears to be only weakly differentiated or barely differentiated at all: a fairly uniform mixture of rock and ice that never fully sorted itself out. Its surface is the most heavily cratered in the solar system, saturated with impacts, ancient and undisturbed — a face that has recorded 4 billion years of bombardment because nothing has ever resurfaced it. Callisto is the **control that stayed dead.** And the reason it stayed dead is not composition — it has the same icy makeup as active Ganymede — but its orbital situation. Callisto sits *outside* the resonant lock that drives its three siblings, so it feels almost no tidal heating. It is the moon where you get to see what a giant-planet satellite looks like when the tidal engine is switched off: cold, undifferentiated, cratered, still.

{{image: Callisto (moon) | Callisto, the outermost Galilean and the most heavily cratered body known — a saturated, ancient surface never resurfaced. Half ice like Ganymede, but only weakly differentiated and tidally cold, it is the experiment's control: the moon that stayed dead.}}

### Reading the two axes off the four moons

Set the four side by side and the design of the experiment becomes explicit.

- **Composition tracks distance.** Io 3.53 → Europa 3.01 → Ganymede 1.94 → Callisto 1.83. Denser (rockier) close in, lighter (icier) far out, monotonic. This is the snow-line thermometer, and it is fixed at formation.
- **Activity tracks tides.** Io and Europa and Ganymede are locked in the **Laplace resonance** — their orbital periods run in the exact ratio 1 : 2 : 4, so they repeatedly line up and pump each other's orbits slightly elliptical, and that forced eccentricity is what feeds tidal heating. Callisto is outside the resonance, its orbit is left nearly circular, and it is cold. Io roars, Europa's ocean stays liquid, Ganymede's interior stays warm and layered; Callisto does none of it.

The two axes are orthogonal, and the four moons happen to sample the corners of the grid. Ganymede and Callisto have nearly identical *composition* and utterly different *histories of activity*, which isolates the tidal axis. Io and Callisto have nearly identical *size* and opposite *everything else*, which isolates almost everything. You could not design a better teaching set if you tried, and nobody did — Jupiter simply had four large moons and an orbital resonance, and the result is a controlled experiment sitting 630 million kilometres away, waiting to be read.

One caution the moment-of-inertia measurement makes sharp. The claim that Callisto is undifferentiated and Ganymede is differentiated is not read from density — they have nearly the same density — but from how the mass is arranged inside, which a passing spacecraft measures through the moon's gravity as a **moment-of-inertia factor** $C/MR^2$. A uniform sphere gives $0.4$; a body with its dense material concentrated at the centre gives less. Galileo's flybys returned about $0.31$ for Ganymede (strongly differentiated, dense core at the middle) and about $0.355$ for Callisto (much closer to uniform, only partly sorted). Same recipe, different cooking: Ganymede got hot enough, early enough, to let its rock sink and its ice float; Callisto accreted slowly and coldly enough that rock and ice stayed stirred together. Two moons of one composition, two different thermal histories — the second axis again, written in the interior.

## The moon that makes its own magnetism

Ganymede deserves a paragraph on its own, because it does something that, when you first hear it, sounds like a category error. **It generates its own magnetic field** — an intrinsic, dynamo-driven, planetary-style dipole — and it does so *inside* the magnetic field of Jupiter.

The gas giants chapter laid out the recipe for a planetary dynamo: a region of electrically conducting fluid, convecting, inside a rotating body. Earth manages it with a churning iron core. Ganymede, improbably, manages it too — its metallic core, molten enough and convecting enough, drives a dipole with a surface equatorial field of around 700 nanotesla, several times the strength of the ambient Jovian field at Ganymede's orbit. The consequence is spectacular: Ganymede carves out a little magnetosphere of its own, a magnetic bubble nested inside Jupiter's vast one, the only known instance of a moon with its own magnetic cavity. When Galileo flew through it, the signature was unmistakable — a moon, 2,634 kilometres in radius, behaving like a small planet in the magnetic sense, complete with its own field lines and its own trapped particles.

There is a second, subtler gift in Ganymede's magnetism, and it is a piece of ocean detection so elegant it belongs here even though the ocean-worlds chapter owns the topic. Ganymede has **auroral ovals** — belts of glowing gas above its poles, excited where charged particles rain down along the field lines, and imaged by Hubble. As Jupiter's tilted magnetic field sweeps past Ganymede every orbit, it drives those auroral belts to rock back and forth. How far they should rock depends on what is inside the moon, and here is the trick: a subsurface **salty, electrically conducting ocean** responds to Jupiter's changing field by generating its own opposing field, which pushes back on the rocking and *damps* it. The observed rocking of Ganymede's aurorae is far smaller than a dry interior would allow — the ovals swing by only about two degrees where a rock-and-ice interior with no ocean would give six. That reduction is the ocean, betraying itself from the outside, in the sway of a glow above the poles. A moon generating its own field inside a planet's field, and the wobble of its aurorae reporting the ocean beneath its ice: Ganymede is a small planet doing planetary things, and it advertises its hidden sea in the process.

## Saturn's family: a different architecture

Now cross to Saturn, and the first thing that strikes you is how *different* the arrangement is — and that difference is itself informative about how these systems assemble.

Jupiter's satellite system is dominated by four big moons in a tidy sequence. Saturn's is not. Saturn has exactly one giant, **Titan**, which alone carries more than 90 percent of the mass orbiting the planet and which — with its thick atmosphere and its methane cycle — has a chapter to itself. Below Titan there is no second or third giant. Instead there is a **family of mid-sized icy moons**, none of them close to Titan's mass, arranged in a graded series: Mimas, Enceladus, Tethys, Dione, Rhea, and, out on its own, Iapetus. Where Jupiter built four large children and stopped, Saturn built one enormous child and a brood of smaller icy ones. The contrast is not cosmetic; it is a clue that circumplanetary disks do not all run the same way, and the reason they differ — how much mass the disk carried, how fast it was fed, whether a single body ran away with the accretion or the mass stayed parcelled out — is a live question in how satellite systems form.

The mid-sized moons share one gross property that sets them apart from the Galileans: they are **icy through and through.** Their densities cluster low — Tethys near 0.98 g/cm³, barely denser than water and so nearly pure ice; Rhea about 1.24; Dione about 1.48; Enceladus about 1.61; Mimas about 1.15. Saturn formed farther from the Sun than Jupiter, in a colder part of the nebula, and its disk was colder still, so its moons condensed with far more ice and far less rock than Jupiter's inner two. There is no Io analogue here, no dry ball of silicate — the coldest disk makes the iciest moons. But within that icy family, the individuals are anything but uniform, and the highlights are worth walking.

**Enceladus**, only about 500 kilometres across, is the family's live wire. From fractures near its south pole — the "tiger stripes" — it fires **plumes of water vapour and ice grains** hundreds of kilometres into space, feeding one of Saturn's rings, and Cassini flew a spacecraft directly through the spray and tasted salt and organic molecules. Beneath the ice is a global ocean, kept liquid by tidal flexing, and Enceladus is a second flagship of the ocean-worlds and tidal-heating stories, which is where the plume chemistry and the heat budget are properly worked out. For the comparative set, the lesson is the one Io already taught in a different key: a small, icy, unremarkable-looking moon becomes spectacularly active not because of what it is made of but because of the tidal state it is in — here, a 2 : 1 resonance with its larger neighbour Dione that forces its orbit slightly elliptical and drives the heating.

{{image: Enceladus | Enceladus firing plumes of water vapour and ice from fractures near its south pole. A 500-kilometre ball of ice made active not by its composition but by tidal flexing, it feeds one of Saturn's rings and hides a global ocean beneath its shell.}}

**Iapetus**, farthest out of the mid-sized moons and orbiting on a strangely tilted path, is the family's oddball, and it is odd in two independent ways. First, it is **two-toned** to a degree no other body in the solar system matches: its leading hemisphere is as dark as coal, its trailing hemisphere as bright as snow, a contrast of more than tenfold in reflectivity across a single moon. The mechanism is a runaway: dark dust, probably drifting in from Saturn's distant outer moon Phoebe, coats the leading face; the darkened side absorbs more sunlight, warms, and its surface ice sublimates and migrates to the colder bright side, which grows brighter and cleaner as the dark side grows darker — thermal segregation feeding on itself until the moon is painted in two colours. Second, Iapetus wears an **equatorial ridge**: a wall of mountains running along its equator for more than a thousand kilometres, in places twenty kilometres high, so precisely aligned with the equator that it makes the moon look like a walnut. Its origin is genuinely unsettled — a fossil of an early, much faster spin, a collapsed ring of its own, or an upwelling along the equator — and no explanation is yet secure. Iapetus's low density, near 1.09, marks it as almost all ice, consistent with the cold outer disk; its two faces and its ridge mark it as a world that has kept secrets.

{{image: Iapetus (moon) | Iapetus, with a leading hemisphere as dark as coal and a trailing hemisphere as bright as snow, and a mountainous ridge running exactly along its equator. Its two-tone face is a runaway of thermal migration; the origin of the ridge remains unsettled.}}

**Mimas**, the innermost and smallest of the classical mid-sized moons, is dominated by a single enormous crater, **Herschel**, 139 kilometres across on a moon only 396 kilometres in diameter — a third of the moon's width, an impact that came close to shattering it and left it looking uncannily like a certain fictional battle station. For decades Mimas seemed geologically dead, a cratered iceball. Then careful tracking of a tiny wobble in its rotation, its **libration**, turned up a surprise: the wobble is larger than a frozen-solid interior would produce, and the leading interpretation is a **hidden ocean** beneath tens of kilometres of ice — a young one, perhaps only recently thawed. If it holds, Mimas is the least likely ocean world imaginable, a small cratered moon with no visible activity concealing liquid water, and a warning that a dead-looking surface is not proof of a dead interior.

**Hyperion**, finally, is the family's clown and its physics lesson. It is a small, irregular, porous body — so under-dense, near 0.54 g/cm³, that it must be nearly half empty space, a rubble pile of ice — and it **tumbles chaotically.** Most moons are tidally locked, one face forever toward their planet, spinning as regularly as a clock. Hyperion does not; its rotation is genuinely chaotic in the technical sense, its spin axis and rate changing unpredictably from orbit to orbit, driven by its elongated shape and its resonant, eccentric orbit next to Titan. Point a camera at Hyperion on two successive passes and you cannot predict which way it will be facing. It is the clearest solar-system example of deterministic chaos in a rotating body — the same mathematics that limits weather forecasting, written into the tumble of a moon.

## Composition by distance, activity by tide

Step back from the individuals and the whole giant-moon zoo resolves onto the two axes the Galilean experiment isolated.

The **first axis is composition, and it is set once, at formation, by distance.** A moon's density records the temperature of the disk where it grew: rocky if it condensed inside its planet's snow line, icy if it condensed outside. This is why Jupiter's inner moons are rock and its outer moons are ice, why Saturn's colder disk made an entirely icy family, why the density gradient of the Galileans reproduces the rock-then-ice arrangement of the solar system at large. It is a fossil axis. Nothing a moon does later changes its bulk composition; Io cannot acquire ice and Callisto cannot shed it.

The **second axis is activity, and it is set continuously, in the present, by tides.** Whether a moon is a live world or a dead one depends not on what it is made of but on how hard its planet is flexing it right now, which depends on its orbital eccentricity, which depends on whether it is locked in a resonance that keeps that eccentricity pumped up. Io and Europa and Ganymede are in the Laplace resonance and are alive; Callisto is outside it and is dead. Enceladus is in resonance with Dione and erupts; Mimas is not similarly locked and only *maybe* hides a young ocean. This axis is contingent and changeable — resonances can be entered and left over a moon's history, and a world can be switched on or off.

Keep the two apart and the apparent paradoxes dissolve. Why is dry rocky Io the most active world and icy Callisto the deadest, when ice is the more volatile stuff? Because activity is not about composition at all — it is about tides, and Io is in the resonance while Callisto is out of it. Why are Ganymede and Callisto, twins in density, so different inside? Same reason. Why does 500-kilometre Enceladus erupt while larger Mimas mostly does not? Tidal state, not size or composition. The single most useful habit this chapter can leave you with is the reflex to ask, of any moon, two separate questions and never confuse their answers: *what is it made of* — answered by distance at formation — and *what is it doing* — answered by its tidal state now.

That reflex is also the bridge to the neighbouring chapters. The mechanism behind the second axis, how orbital flexing turns into heat and how a resonance sustains the eccentricity that feeds it, is the tidal-heating chapter's to derive. What that heat does when it reaches liquid water, and how we detect an ocean under kilometres of ice, is the ocean-worlds chapter's. Titan, Saturn's one giant, and its atmosphere and lakes, is its own. And the moons this chapter set aside at the start — the captured irregulars on their tilted backward orbits, and Neptune's stolen Triton — are the subject of the chapter on how a planet acquires moons it did not make. This chapter's job was the comparative frame: the giant satellite systems as miniature solar systems, and the Galileans as the controlled experiment that separates the two things a moon can be.

## Further reading

The imagery and data underlying this chapter are almost entirely the product of two orbiters whose archives are public: **Galileo** at Jupiter, which returned the flyby gravity data behind the moment-of-inertia measurements and the first strong evidence for the moons' interior structure, and **Cassini** at Saturn, which mapped the entire mid-sized family, flew through the Enceladus plume, and tracked Mimas's telltale libration. The Jupiter system is now being revisited by **Juno**'s extended mission and, more decisively, by **Europa Clipper** and Europe's **JUICE**, the latter built to end its life in orbit around Ganymede — the first spacecraft ever to orbit a moon other than our own. For the density-and-formation argument at the heart of the chapter, any modern planetary-science text's treatment of satellite formation and the Canup–Ward gas-starved disk model lays out the reasoning; the mission science pages carry the numbers.

## Problems

*Constants and data you may need: gravitational constant $G = 6.674\times10^{-11}$ SI. Densities to assume for two-component modelling: rock $\rho_r = 3.5$ g/cm³, water ice $\rho_i = 1.0$ g/cm³. Galilean data: Io ($R=1{,}822$ km, $\rho=3.53$), Europa ($R=1{,}561$ km, $\rho=3.01$), Ganymede ($R=2{,}634$ km, $M=1.482\times10^{23}$ kg, $\rho=1.94$), Callisto ($R=2{,}410$ km, $\rho=1.83$). Mercury: $R=2{,}440$ km, $M=3.30\times10^{23}$ kg, $\rho=5.43$. Moment-of-inertia factors: Ganymede $0.31$, Callisto $0.355$; uniform sphere $0.40$. Problem 2 (reading the density sequence) and problem 5 (the two axes) are the conceptual core; problem 9 has no settled answer.*

**1 — The census.** **(a)** Using round counts — Jupiter ~95, Saturn ~140, Uranus ~28, Neptune ~16, all other bodies ~3 — what fraction of the solar system's moons orbit the four giants? **(b)** In one sentence, state the physical reason this fraction is so lopsided rather than moons being spread evenly among the planets. **(c)** Why does the fact that regular moons orbit *prograde, near-circular, near-equatorial* support the claim that they formed in a disk rather than being captured?

**2 — Reading the density sequence (conceptual core).** The Galilean densities are Io 3.53, Europa 3.01, Ganymede 1.94, Callisto 1.83 g/cm³. **(a)** State plainly what the *outward* fall of this sequence tells you, and name the single formation quantity it is a fossil of. **(b)** Why is it legitimate to treat this as a controlled experiment — what is being held fixed, and what is being varied? **(c)** The solar system's planets also fall from dense-rocky (inner) to light-icy (outer). Explain why the two gradients have the same cause without being the same event.

**3 — Uncompressed density and ice fraction.** Model each icy moon as a mixture of rock ($\rho_r=3.5$) and water ice ($\rho_i=1.0$), with ice mass fraction $f$, so that $1/\rho = f/\rho_i + (1-f)/\rho_r$. **(a)** Solve for $f$ in terms of $\rho$. **(b)** Compute the ice mass fraction of Ganymede ($\rho=1.94$) and Callisto ($\rho=1.83$). **(c)** Compute it for Europa ($\rho=3.01$) and comment on what "a few percent water" looks like as a moon. **(d)** Apply the formula to Io ($\rho=3.53$). What does the result (and its awkwardness) tell you about the model's limits?

**4 — Ganymede versus Mercury.** Ganymede is the largest moon in the solar system; Mercury is a planet. **(a)** Compare their radii: which is bigger, and by what percentage in radius and in volume? **(b)** Compute each body's mean density from the data given and confirm the table values. **(c)** Compute the surface gravity $g = GM/R^2$ of each. Mercury is the smaller body — why is its surface gravity so much larger? **(d)** In one sentence, resolve the apparent paradox that the "largest moon" is outmassed more than two to one by a "small planet."

**5 — The two axes (conceptual core).** A student says: "Io is dry rock and Callisto is half ice, yet Io is the most active world in the solar system and Callisto is dead — so ice must suppress activity and rock must promote it." **(a)** Diagnose the error precisely. **(b)** State the two independent axes that actually organise the giant-planet moons, and which formation-or-present quantity sets each. **(c)** Use Ganymede and Callisto — near-identical in density — to show the two axes are independent. **(d)** Predict what would happen to Callisto's activity if some orbital rearrangement captured it into the Laplace resonance, and what would *not* change.

**6 — Ganymede's magnetism and its aurora.** **(a)** State the general recipe for a planetary dynamo and argue that Ganymede plausibly meets it. **(b)** Ganymede's field is nested inside Jupiter's much larger one; what makes this unique among moons? **(c)** Ganymede's auroral ovals rock by only about $2°$ as Jupiter's field sweeps past, where a dry interior would give about $6°$. Explain, from electromagnetic induction, why a subsurface salty ocean reduces the rocking. **(d)** Why is this a *cleaner* ocean detector than simply looking at the surface for cracks or plumes?

**7 — Saturn's icy family.** **(a)** Tethys has density about 0.98 g/cm³, essentially that of water ice. What is its approximate rock fraction, and what does that imply about where and how cold it formed? **(b)** Contrast Saturn's architecture (one giant plus a graded mid-sized icy family) with Jupiter's (four large moons). Name one thing that contrast plausibly tells you about the two circumplanetary disks. **(c)** Iapetus's leading hemisphere is more than ten times darker than its trailing hemisphere. Explain the runaway that produces and then amplifies such a contrast. **(d)** Why is Iapetus's low density (~1.09) consistent with the rest of the family even though its surface is so anomalous?

**8 — Small moons, opposite fates.** Enceladus (~500 km) erupts continuously; Mimas (~400 km) looks dead but may hide a young ocean; Hyperion (~270 km) tumbles chaotically. **(a)** Enceladus and Mimas are both small icy moons close to Saturn, yet only one is obviously active. What single factor most plausibly separates them, and how does it connect to the second axis of problem 5? **(b)** Mimas's oversized libration is the evidence for its possible ocean. Why is a *dead-looking, heavily cratered* surface not evidence against an interior ocean? **(c)** Almost every moon is tidally locked and spins like a clock; Hyperion does not. Name the two ingredients that make its rotation chaotic, and say what "chaotic" means operationally for someone trying to photograph it.

**9 — Open problem: why these two architectures?** Jupiter built four large moons in a density sequence; Saturn built one giant and a family of mid-sized icy moons. **(a)** Sketch two distinct hypotheses for why one circumplanetary disk yields four comparable moons and another yields one dominant moon plus a graded family. **(b)** For each, state what present-day observable would count as evidence. **(c)** Uranus has a family of mid-sized moons and no giant; Neptune's system is dominated by captured Triton and is otherwise sparse. How do those two systems bear on your hypotheses? **(d)** Design the single measurement or population study you would most want, and say what each hypothesis predicts for it.

## Worked answers

**1 — The census.** **(a)** Giants: $95+140+28+16 = 279$. Everything else: $\approx 3$. Total $\approx 282$. Fraction at the giants $= 279/282 = 0.989$, about **99 percent.** **(b)** Because moons in this abundance are what a body gets only if it was massive enough to hold a gas-and-dust disk of its own during formation, and only the four giants ever were; the inner planets were too small and too warm, so their handful of moons arrived by impact or capture, not by disk accretion. **(c)** A disk is flat, rotating in one sense, and centred on the planet's equator, so bodies that condense out of it inherit those properties — prograde motion, low inclination, near-circular orbits. Capture, by contrast, grabs whatever passes at whatever angle, producing tilted, eccentric, often retrograde orbits. The three "regular" signatures are therefore the fingerprint of in-situ disk formation, which is why they, not the captured irregulars, carry the formation record.

**2 — Reading the density sequence.** **(a)** The outward fall means the moons grow steadily *icier* with distance from Jupiter: rock-dominated close in, ice-rich far out. It is a fossil of the **temperature profile of Jupiter's circumplanetary disk** at the time the moons condensed — a snow line drawn around Jupiter, with rock condensing in the hot inner disk and water ice condensing only in the cold outer disk. Density is functioning as a thermometer. **(b)** Held fixed: parent body, age, formation mechanism, broad chemical reservoir — all four are disk children of one Jupiter, formed together. Varied: principally formation distance (and, separately, present tidal state). With almost everything controlled, the density-versus-distance trend can be attributed to the one variable that changed, which is exactly what makes it experiment-like rather than anecdotal. **(c)** Both gradients have the *same cause* — a central hot object drawing a temperature profile across its disk, so that solids condense rocky where it is warm and icy where it is cold, and bodies inherit the local mix. But they are not the *same event*: one is the Sun heating the solar nebula out to ~2.7 AU, the other is proto-Jupiter heating its own circumplanetary disk out to a few of its radii, 4.5 billion years ago, on a scale ten thousand times smaller. The Galilean sequence is a miniature re-run of the same physics, which is precisely why it looks like the solar system in small.

**3 — Uncompressed density and ice fraction.** **(a)** From $1/\rho = f/\rho_i + (1-f)/\rho_r$, expand: $1/\rho = f/\rho_i + 1/\rho_r - f/\rho_r = 1/\rho_r + f(1/\rho_i - 1/\rho_r)$. Solve:
$$f = \frac{1/\rho - 1/\rho_r}{1/\rho_i - 1/\rho_r}.$$
With $\rho_i=1.0$ and $\rho_r=3.5$, the denominator is $1 - 0.2857 = 0.7143$. **(b)** Ganymede, $\rho=1.94$: $1/\rho = 0.5155$; $f = (0.5155 - 0.2857)/0.7143 = 0.2298/0.7143 = 0.32$, about **32 percent ice by mass** — and since ice is much less dense, that is roughly half the moon by *volume*, matching "half rock, half ice." Callisto, $\rho=1.83$: $1/\rho = 0.5464$; $f = (0.5464-0.2857)/0.7143 = 0.365$, about **37 percent ice by mass.** Callisto is slightly icier than Ganymede, consistent with forming farther out. **(c)** Europa, $\rho=3.01$: $1/\rho = 0.3322$; $f = (0.3322-0.2857)/0.7143 = 0.065$, about **6–7 percent water by mass.** That is a rock world with a thin outer water layer — a shell of order 100 km on a 1,561-km body — which is exactly Europa: silicate through and through, wrapped in ice and ocean. **(d)** Io, $\rho=3.53$: $1/\rho=0.2833 < 0.2857 = 1/\rho_r$, so the formula returns a *slightly negative* $f$. The awkwardness is the point: Io is denser than our assumed pure rock, so a two-component rock-plus-ice model cannot fit it at all — there is no ice to speak of, and the excess density signals a **denser component**, iron, in a differentiated metallic core. The model's job was to estimate ice fraction; a negative answer is the model correctly reporting "no ice, and in fact something heavier than plain rock." Real inferences replace the fixed $\rho_r=3.5$ with a rock-plus-metal interior and account for self-compression, but the crude model already sorts the four moons correctly.

**4 — Ganymede versus Mercury.** **(a)** Ganymede $R=2{,}634$ km, Mercury $R=2{,}440$ km, so **Ganymede is bigger**, by $2634/2440 = 1.080$, about **8 percent in radius.** In volume that is $1.080^3 = 1.26$, about **26 percent more voluminous.** **(b)** Ganymede: $V = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi(2.634\times10^6)^3 = 7.66\times10^{19}$ m³; $\rho = 1.482\times10^{23}/7.66\times10^{19} = 1{,}935$ kg/m³ ≈ **1.94 g/cm³.** Mercury: $V = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi(2.440\times10^6)^3 = 6.08\times10^{19}$ m³; $\rho = 3.30\times10^{23}/6.08\times10^{19} = 5{,}430$ kg/m³ ≈ **5.43 g/cm³.** Confirmed. **(c)** Ganymede: $g = (6.674\times10^{-11})(1.482\times10^{23})/(2.634\times10^6)^2 = 9.89\times10^{12}/6.94\times10^{12} = \mathbf{1.43}$ m/s². Mercury: $g = (6.674\times10^{-11})(3.30\times10^{23})/(2.440\times10^6)^2 = 2.20\times10^{13}/5.95\times10^{12} = \mathbf{3.70}$ m/s². Mercury's surface gravity is **2.6 times** Ganymede's despite Mercury being the smaller body, because $g \propto \rho R$ and Mercury is nearly three times as dense — a huge iron core packed under a thin rock mantle — so even at slightly smaller radius it holds far more mass beneath your feet. **(d)** The largest moon is bigger in *size* but Ganymede is half ice while Mercury is mostly iron, so the same volume carries less than half the mass — "largest" is a statement about radius, "most massive" is a statement about density times volume, and for icy-versus-metallic bodies the two need not agree.

**5 — The two axes.** **(a)** The error is conflating *composition* with *activity* — treating "what a moon is made of" and "what a moon is doing" as one variable when they are two. Ice does not suppress activity and rock does not promote it; Io's makeup and Io's volcanism have nothing to do with each other. **(b)** The two independent axes are: **composition**, set once at formation by distance from the planet (the snow-line thermometer — density records where the moon condensed); and **activity**, set continuously in the present by tidal heating, which depends on orbital eccentricity, which depends on whether the moon is trapped in a resonance that keeps the eccentricity pumped. Composition is a fossil; activity is a live, changeable state. **(c)** Ganymede and Callisto have near-identical density (~1.9), so they sit at essentially the same point on the composition axis — both half-ice — yet Ganymede is differentiated, warm, ocean-bearing, and magnetised while Callisto is undifferentiated, cold, and dead. The only thing separating them is tidal state: Ganymede is in the Laplace resonance, Callisto is outside it. Same composition, opposite activity — which is exactly what "two independent axes" means. **(d)** If Callisto were captured into the resonance, its orbit would be forced eccentric, tidal flexing would begin depositing heat, and over time its interior could warm, differentiate further, and potentially sustain or enlarge a subsurface ocean — it could switch *on*. What would **not** change is its composition: it would still be a half-ice, half-rock body, because bulk makeup is fixed at formation and no orbital rearrangement adds or removes ice. Activity is contingent; composition is not.

**6 — Ganymede's magnetism and its aurora.** **(a)** A planetary dynamo needs a region of electrically conducting fluid, convecting, inside a rotating body. Ganymede is differentiated with an iron-rich metallic core (its low moment-of-inertia factor, 0.31, confirms a dense concentrated centre); if part of that core is molten and convecting, and Ganymede rotates (it is tidally locked but still spins once per orbit), the recipe is met — which is why it is the one moon that pulls it off. **(b)** Ganymede's dipole is strong enough (~700 nT at the surface) to exceed Jupiter's ambient field at its orbit and carve out its own magnetospheric cavity nested inside Jupiter's vast one — the only known moon with a magnetosphere of its own, a small magnetised world embedded in a giant one. **(c)** As Jupiter's tilted field sweeps past, the changing external field induces, by Faraday's law, an electric current in any conducting layer inside the moon; a **salty subsurface ocean** is such a layer. That induced current generates its own magnetic field opposing the change, which partly cancels the sweeping field the aurorae respond to, so the ovals rock less than they would over a non-conducting (dry) interior. Observed rocking ~2° against a dry-interior expectation of ~6° is the induced ocean field doing the damping. **(d)** Because it probes the *interior* directly and globally, independent of surface appearance: cracks or plumes can be absent, buried, ancient, or ambiguous, but electromagnetic induction responds to the presence of a conducting (salty, liquid) layer wherever it is, and the size of the response scales with the ocean's conductivity and depth. It detects the ocean itself, not a surface symptom that may or may not be there.

**7 — Saturn's icy family.** **(a)** With density 0.98 essentially equal to pure ice, the ice mass fraction from the problem-3 formula is near $f = (1/0.98 - 0.2857)/0.7143 = (1.020-0.286)/0.714 = 1.03$ — i.e. within the model's slop, **essentially all ice, negligible rock.** That implies Tethys condensed in a very cold part of Saturn's disk, cold enough that water ice dominated the available solids and little rock was incorporated. **(b)** Jupiter's disk produced four comparable large moons in a graded sequence; Saturn's produced one runaway giant (Titan) plus a family of much smaller icy bodies. That contrast plausibly reflects a difference in how the disks fed and evolved — for instance whether the disk allowed a single body to run away with most of the accreting mass (Saturn) or parcelled the mass into several comparable bodies (Jupiter) — telling us circumplanetary disks are not one-size-fits-all and that the outcome is sensitive to disk mass, resupply rate, and migration. (Any specific, physically reasonable such factor earns the point.) **(c)** A small initial darkening of the leading hemisphere — dark dust drifting in from the outer moon Phoebe and swept up on the leading face — lowers that side's reflectivity, so it absorbs more sunlight and warms; the warmer surface ice sublimates and migrates to the colder, brighter trailing side, which stays clean and grows brighter still. Darker gets warmer gets darker; brighter gets colder gets brighter. The feedback runs away until the two hemispheres are separated by more than tenfold in albedo. **(d)** Density measures *bulk composition*, which for Iapetus is ~all ice (1.09) — entirely consistent with the cold outer disk that made the whole family icy. The two-tone face is a thin *surface* effect from thermal migration of a coating, and the equatorial ridge is a structural oddity; neither changes the bulk ice-to-rock ratio. A moon can be compositionally ordinary and superficially bizarre at the same time.

**8 — Small moons, opposite fates.** **(a)** Tidal state — specifically, whether the moon is in an eccentricity-pumping resonance. Enceladus is locked in a 2 : 1 resonance with Dione, which forces its orbit slightly elliptical and drives strong tidal heating; Mimas is not similarly maintained, so it flexes far less and stays mostly cold. This is the second axis of problem 5 exactly: near-identical small icy moons, opposite activity, separated by tidal forcing rather than by composition or size. **(b)** Because a heavily cratered surface only tells you the *outside* has not been resurfaced recently; it says nothing about whether liquid water sits tens of kilometres below the ice. Mimas's oversized libration — a rotational wobble larger than a frozen-through interior would allow — probes the interior mechanically, and it is consistent with a decoupled ocean under a rigid shell. A dead face can cap a live interior, which is precisely why Mimas is a cautionary example. **(c)** Two ingredients: an **elongated (non-spherical) shape**, which gives the planet's gravity a strong handle to torque the moon's spin, and a **sufficiently eccentric orbit** (maintained by Hyperion's resonance with Titan), which makes that torque vary strongly around each orbit. Together they make the rotation chaotic — meaning it is deterministic but exquisitely sensitive to initial conditions, so its spin rate and axis are effectively unpredictable more than a little while ahead. Operationally: you cannot forecast which face Hyperion will present on your next flyby, because tiny uncertainties in its current spin blow up over an orbit or two.

**9 — Open problem: why these two architectures?** **(a)** Two hypotheses. **(i) Disk mass and feeding rate:** if a circumplanetary disk is continuously resupplied and moons form and migrate inward and are lost in succession, the *last* survivors reflect the endgame — Jupiter's may have ended with several comparable bodies surviving in resonance, while Saturn's may have let one body (Titan) accrete most of the mass before the disk shut off, leaving smaller bodies to form afterward from what remained. **(ii) A giant impact or late reassembly:** Saturn's mid-sized family (and its young, bright rings) might be relatively recent, the debris of a disrupted larger moon or a series of collisions, rather than a pristine formation sequence — a fundamentally different origin from Jupiter's orderly four. **(b)** For (i): the *masses, spacings, and resonant structure* of the surviving moons, plus disk models constrained by the moons' compositions and the planet's spin — a resupplied-and-lost disk predicts specific survivor patterns and a strong composition-versus-distance gradient. For (ii): the *ages of surfaces and rings* (young, unbattered surfaces or a demonstrably young ring system argue for recent, collisional origin), and dynamical evidence of past disruption (odd orbital inclinations, families of fragments, non-equilibrium spins like Hyperion's). **(c)** Uranus's graded mid-sized family with no giant resembles Saturn's brood *minus* the giant, suggesting the "one dominant moon" outcome is not universal and that whatever made Titan need not fire — favouring the view that the presence or absence of a runaway giant is contingent on disk conditions rather than inevitable. Neptune's sparse system dominated by *captured* Triton is different in kind: Triton's capture would have dynamically wrecked any pre-existing regular system, so Neptune tells you less about disk formation and more about how catastrophic a late capture is — a reminder to separate disk-born architecture from later disruption. **(d)** The measurement most worth having is a **population study of satellite-system architectures with well-determined moon masses, compositions (densities), and surface/ring ages across all four giants**, ideally extended to giant exoplanets' moons if they ever become detectable. The orderly-disk hypothesis predicts that architecture correlates smoothly with disk properties (planet mass, formation location, spin) and that composition-versus-distance gradients are generic and old; the collisional/recent-origin hypothesis predicts *young* surfaces or rings and stochastic, hard-to-predict architectures that do not correlate cleanly with disk parameters. A single system cannot decide between "the disk always does this" and "a rare event happened here" — as with the giant planets' dilute cores, the question is about a population, and it needs a population to answer.$astroMoons_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
