with q as (
  select $astroMoons_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter calls the Galilean moons a 'controlled experiment.' What is the single variable that is principally allowed to vary across Io, Europa, Ganymede, and Callisto, while parent, age, and formation mechanism are held fixed?", "options": ["The size of each moon, which independently determines how much internal heat it retains", "How far from Jupiter each moon formed, which set the disk temperature it condensed at and therefore its rock-to-ice composition", "The chemical reservoir each moon drew from, since each formed from a chemically distinct pocket of the solar nebula", "The age of each moon, since the outer moons finished accreting hundreds of millions of years after the inner ones"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The power of the Galilean set is that nature nearly performed a controlled experiment: four large moons sharing one parent (Jupiter), one age (all ~4.5 billion years old, formed together), and one formation mechanism (condensation in Jupiter's circumplanetary disk). What differs principally is formation distance, which fixed the local disk temperature and hence how much ice a moon could incorporate. Size is a consequence, not the controlled input, and the chapter deliberately separates size from activity (Io and our Moon are near-twins in size yet utterly different in activity). The moons share a common chemical reservoir rather than distinct ones, and they are the same age, not staggered. Holding almost everything else fixed is exactly what lets the density-versus-distance trend be attributed to the one variable that changed."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Reading the Galilean bulk densities from Io to Callisto (3.53, 3.01, 1.94, 1.83 g/cm3), what does the smooth outward fall record, and why?", "options": ["It records the temperature profile of proto-Jupiter's own circumplanetary disk, because the hot young planet drew a snow line across its disk so rock condensed close in and water ice condensed only farther out", "It records how much tidal heating each moon has received, because heating over billions of years boils off ice and leaves the inner moons denser", "It records the order in which the moons were captured by Jupiter, with the densest bodies captured first from the inner solar system", "It records the strength of Jupiter's magnetic field at each orbit, which stripped volatile ices more effectively from the closer moons"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The density gradient is a fossil thermometer. A newly formed Jupiter radiated the gravitational energy of its assembly, so its circumplanetary disk was hot close in and cold far out, drawing a snow line of its own just as the young Sun drew one near 2.7 AU. Inside that snow line only anhydrous rock and metal could condense (Io, 3.53, is bone-dry rock); outside it water ice condensed in bulk, roughly doubling the solid mass and yielding half-ice moons (Callisto, 1.83). Europa (3.01) and Ganymede (1.94) straddle the transition. Composition is set once, at formation, by distance; it is not tidal (that is the separate activity axis), not capture (the regulars are disk-born, not captured), and not magnetic stripping. The gradient is the disk's temperature profile from 4.5 billion years ago read off four moons in a row."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Io (density 3.53, dry rock) is the most volcanically active body in the solar system, while Callisto (density 1.83, half ice) is the deadest. A student concludes that rock promotes activity and ice suppresses it. Why is this wrong?", "options": ["Because Io is actually much larger than Callisto, and it is size, not composition, that drives its greater internal heat and volcanism", "Because Callisto's ice is a better thermal insulator, so it traps heat and should in fact be more active than the student expects", "Because activity is not about composition at all: it is set by tidal heating, and Io sits inside the Laplace resonance that pumps its eccentricity while Callisto sits outside it", "Because Io's volcanism is powered by radioactive decay of elements concentrated in its silicate rock, which icy Callisto lacks"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The error is conflating two orthogonal axes: what a moon is made of (composition, set at formation by distance) and what a moon is doing (activity, set in the present by tides). A ball of rock Io's size should have formed cold and stayed cold like our Moon; its fury comes entirely from tidal flexing along its forced-eccentric orbit. Io, Europa, and Ganymede are locked in the Laplace resonance (periods in the ratio 1:2:4) that repeatedly aligns them and keeps their orbits slightly elliptical, feeding tidal heat. Callisto sits outside the resonance, its orbit stays nearly circular, and it feels almost no tidal heating -- the control that stayed dead. Io and Callisto are near-twins in size, so size is not the driver; and the deadest moon being the icy one shows ice does not suppress activity. The reflex the chapter wants is to ask 'made of what?' and 'doing what?' as two separate questions."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Ganymede and Callisto have nearly identical bulk densities (1.94 vs 1.83), yet the chapter insists one is differentiated and the other is not. How is that difference actually measured, and what does it show?", "options": ["By comparing their surface crater counts, which show Ganymede has been resurfaced by internal heat while Callisto has not", "By the moment-of-inertia factor C/MR^2 from spacecraft gravity flybys: about 0.31 for Ganymede (dense core concentrated at center) versus about 0.355 for Callisto (much closer to a uniform 0.4 sphere)", "By measuring each moon's magnetic field strength, since only a differentiated moon with a metallic core can generate a dynamo", "By their bulk densities directly, since Ganymede's slightly higher density proves its rock has all sunk to the center"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Differentiation is about how mass is arranged inside, not the average density, which is why two moons of nearly identical density can differ. A passing spacecraft measures the moment-of-inertia factor C/MR^2 from the moon's gravity: a uniform sphere gives 0.4, and a body with dense material concentrated at the center gives less. Galileo's flybys returned about 0.31 for Ganymede (strongly differentiated, dense core at the middle) and about 0.355 for Callisto (close to uniform, only partly sorted). Same recipe, different cooking: Ganymede got hot enough early enough for its rock to sink and ice to float, while Callisto accreted slowly and coldly enough that rock and ice stayed stirred together. Crater counts and magnetism are downstream symptoms; density cannot distinguish them because it is nearly the same. This is the interior signature of the second (tidal/thermal-history) axis."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How does Saturn's satellite architecture differ from Jupiter's, and what does the chapter say that contrast plausibly signals?", "options": ["Saturn has four large moons in a density sequence just like Jupiter, showing that all circumplanetary disks assemble the same way", "Saturn has one dominant giant (Titan, >90 percent of the orbiting mass) plus a family of mid-sized icy moons, a contrast suggesting circumplanetary disks do not all run the same way", "Saturn has only tiny captured irregular moons because its rings consumed all the disk material before regular moons could form", "Saturn's mid-sized moons are rockier than Jupiter's because Saturn's disk was hotter, having formed closer to the Sun"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Where Jupiter built four large children in a tidy density sequence and stopped, Saturn built one enormous child, Titan, carrying more than 90 percent of the orbiting mass, plus a brood of mid-sized icy moons (Mimas, Enceladus, Tethys, Dione, Rhea, Iapetus). This is not cosmetic: it signals that circumplanetary disks do not all run the same way, and the reason -- how much mass the disk held, how fast it was fed, whether one body ran away with the accretion or the mass stayed parcelled out -- is a live question in satellite formation. Saturn formed farther from the Sun in a colder nebula, so its disk was colder, making an entirely icy family with no Io analogue (densities cluster low, Tethys near 0.98 is nearly pure ice). So the disk was colder, not hotter, and the moons are icier, not rockier; the regulars are disk-born, not captured."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain in what sense a giant planet's circumplanetary disk is 'a solar system in miniature,' and how the two axes the chapter isolates -- composition set by formation distance, and activity set by present tides -- together explain why dry rocky Io is the most active world while icy Callisto is the deadest. Use the chapter's actual numbers.", "rubric": "A strong answer has three parts. (1) The miniature-solar-system claim: when Jupiter finished runaway accretion it was a hot, luminous, still-collapsing body surrounded by a circumplanetary disk of gas and dust, flattened by rotation and warmed from the center by the planet's own heat. Every process that built the planets from the Sun's disk operated again one tier down: condensation of solids set by local temperature, accretion into larger bodies, migration, resonance, and a central luminous object drawing a temperature gradient. The regular moons are 'disk children,' evidenced by prograde, near-circular, near-equatorial orbits; the leading model is Canup and Ward's gas-starved disk. (2) Axis one, composition by distance: proto-Jupiter drew its own snow line (ice condenses below ~150 K) across its disk because it was hot. Inside it only anhydrous rock/metal condensed; outside, water ice condensed in bulk (~doubling solid mass). Hence the monotonic density fall Io 3.53 -> Europa 3.01 -> Ganymede 1.94 -> Callisto 1.83 g/cm3, a fossil thermometer of the disk's 4.5-billion-year-old temperature profile, echoing the solar system's own rock-inside/ice-outside arrangement. Composition is fixed at formation and cannot change. (3) Axis two, activity by tides: activity depends not on composition but on tidal heating, which depends on orbital eccentricity, which depends on resonance. Io, Europa, Ganymede are locked in the Laplace resonance (periods 1:2:4) that pumps their eccentricities; Callisto sits outside it with a nearly circular orbit and feels almost no tidal heating. Therefore Io, a Moon-sized ball of dry rock that should have formed cold and stayed cold, is instead the most volcanically active body (no craters, plumes hundreds of km high) purely from tidal flexing, while Callisto, half ice like active Ganymede, is the most heavily cratered, undifferentiated, dead 'control.' The synthesis: the two axes are orthogonal, so Io's makeup (a formation fact) and Io's fury (a present-tense tidal fact) have nothing to do with each other -- the apparent paradox (icy Callisto deadest, rocky Io liveliest) dissolves once composition and activity are kept apart. Bonus: noting Ganymede/Callisto near-identical density but opposite activity isolates the tidal axis."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Ganymede is described both as 'the largest moon in the solar system' and as 'a small planet doing planetary things.' Justify both descriptions using the chapter's evidence -- its size relative to Mercury, its interior, its self-generated magnetic field, and the auroral-oval ocean detection -- and explain why 'largest' does not mean 'most massive.'", "rubric": "A strong answer covers: (1) Size: Ganymede's radius is 2,634 km, larger in raw diameter than the planet Mercury (2,440 km) and larger than Titan by a whisker -- the largest moon in the solar system. (2) 'Largest' vs 'most massive': despite being bigger, Ganymede is roughly half ice by mass while Mercury is mostly iron and far denser (5.43 vs 1.94 g/cm3), so Mercury outmasses Ganymede more than two to one; 'largest' is a statement about radius/volume, 'most massive' about density times volume, and for icy-versus-metallic bodies the two need not agree. (3) Interior: Ganymede formed comfortably outside Jupiter's snow line, took its full share of water (~half rock, half ice), and had enough internal heat to differentiate cleanly into a metallic core, rock mantle, and thick ice layers, with a subsurface ocean between ice phases; its low moment-of-inertia factor (~0.31) confirms a dense concentrated core. (4) Self-generated magnetism: uniquely among moons, Ganymede runs its own dynamo -- a molten, convecting metallic core inside a rotating body meets the dynamo recipe -- producing an intrinsic dipole with a surface equatorial field of ~700 nT, several times the ambient Jovian field at its orbit. It carves out its own magnetosphere, a magnetic bubble nested inside Jupiter's vast one, the only known moon with its own magnetic cavity (confirmed by Galileo). (5) Auroral ocean detection: Ganymede has auroral ovals (imaged by Hubble) that rock back and forth as Jupiter's tilted field sweeps past; a subsurface salty, electrically conducting ocean responds by electromagnetic induction, generating an opposing field that damps the rocking. The ovals swing by only ~2 degrees where a dry rock-and-ice interior would give ~6 degrees, and that reduction betrays the hidden ocean from the outside. Conclusion: a moon generating its own field inside a planet's field, differentiated into planetary layers, bigger across than Mercury, and advertising a hidden sea through the sway of its aurorae -- genuinely a small planet doing planetary things, even though 'largest' by size still leaves it less massive than the metallic planet it outspans."}]$astroMoons_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/giant-planets/major-moons', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
